PG0 found the EcowoodsGuide assistant firing zero analytics events while
creating real QuoteRequest rows and booking real Appointments, and the
movement calculator and quote comparator firing none at all. Three tools a
visitor can spend ten minutes inside, invisible to every report — so the
honest answer to "is the assistant worth what it costs to run" was that
nobody could tell.

A HOLE IN MEAS-02, CLOSED

The funnel ledger was wired to /api/leads and /api/appointments. The
assistant's own tools write those same two tables WITHOUT going through
either route: book_measure creates a QuoteRequest and an Appointment in one
transaction, create_quote_request creates a QuoteRequest directly. Every
measure the assistant booked was therefore invisible to /admin/funnel, and
the assistant appeared to produce nothing at all.

book_measure now records BOTH stages, because that one call is both: a lead
was captured and an appointment was booked. Recording only the second would
have shown more appointments than leads — a funnel that reports a stage
converting above 100% and looks like a bug in the arithmetic rather than a
bug in the instrument. create_quote_request records only on the path where a
row actually exists; its DB-failure fallback deliberately has no quoteId,
and a ledger row pointing at nothing counts a lead the funnel can never
follow.

THE EVENT THAT NEVER EXISTED

`commercial_cta` and `realtor_cta` have been in the AnalyticsEvent union
since it was written and have never fired from anywhere. The cause is
structural, not an oversight: /commercial and /realtors are server
components, track() is browser-only, and nobody had built the one-line
client boundary. So the two highest-intent commercial pages on the site
reported nothing while the union looked like coverage.

`TrackedCta` is that boundary and is deliberately the whole of it: a link,
an event name, an optional label. It takes no parameter bag — a tracked
link that accepted one becomes the place somebody eventually puts an email
address.

THE TEST THAT SHOULD HAVE EXISTED FIRST

"Every declared event is fired from somewhere" now walks every .ts/.tsx
under apps/web and fails naming any union member with no call site. Two
events lived in that contract for its whole life without one. A closed
union looks like coverage and nothing checked that the contract and the
code agreed.

WHAT THE NEW EVENTS ARE ALLOWED TO CARRY

assistant_open carries which control opened the panel. assistant_message
carries a turn COUNT — a one-turn session and an eight-turn session are
different products and were previously indistinguishable — and never a word
the visitor typed. The commercial outcomes stay server-side in the ledger,
where they are authoritative and do not depend on consent; these are the
denominator.

movement_calculated and quote_check_compared fire ONCE, on first real
engagement, not on arrival and not per keystroke: a page view is not use of
a calculator, and counting arrivals as usage would have made the least-used
tool on the site look like one of the busiest.

Neither carries a figure. The comparator's page promises three times that
nothing is uploaded and nothing is stored, and it is the one tool where the
visitor is holding another company's commercial document — an event
carrying a total would make that sentence false. Tests assert the parameter
keys against a closed allowlist rather than a substring blocklist, after my
first version failed on `board_mm` and would have forced me to drop a
legitimate preset choice to satisfy a rule nobody meant.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards (verify:css needs postcss from node_modules,
absent here). parse-scan 608 files, zero diagnostics. Every changed file
through the resolving typecheck — which needed two fixes of its own this
round (JSX.ElementChildrenAttribute, and useRef's null overload) before its
TS2741s could be trusted as artifacts rather than findings. 29 new
assertions; 68,204 across the local suite.

